<h3>My current method</h3>

I credit James Hoffman for getting me into coffee at a deeper level, and I find that <a href="https://youtu.be/1oB1oDrDkHM?si=lNaYABwXAL4XODhJ">his approach to the V60</a> produces very nice cups, so I follow that recipe and tweak variables here and there as needed.<br><br>

<b>Ratio:</b> 1:16.7 (250g water to 15g coffee)<br>
<b>Grind size:</b> I'll experiment with new beans, but I default to ~16-18 clicks, so a medium fine to coarse.<br> 
<b>Water temperature:</b> Usually in the 92 to 95 degrees celsius range, depending on the beans.<br>

<ol>
<li>Bloom coffee in about 45g of water for 45 seconds. I gently swirl right after this initial pour to try and make sure all the grounds get saturated.</li>
<li>Pour 50g of water and gently swirl again to make sure the grounds all settle.</li>
<li>Every 10 seconds or so, pour an additional 50g of water until I reach 250g.</li>
</ol>

<b>Target drawdown time:</b> 2.5 to 3 minutes (highly variable)
